mechanism_check.specialized_operation:
FaBC: g(k) = 1 − F_k + k/L + k/32. For F_2 = 0.6, L = 80: g(2) = 0.4875.

Varint: g_v = (1/4)·Σ_r p(r)·b(r) + o, where b(r) = 1/2/3/4–5 bytes for r < 2^7 / 2^14 / 2^21 / beyond, and o is the offset overhead. With 4-byte per-sequence offsets, o = 1/L = 0.0125.

Under the proposal's assumed split (0.6 mass at 1 byte, 0.2 at 2 bytes, 0.2 at 3 bytes): g_v = 1.6/4 + 0.0125 = 0.4125, and g_v/g = 0.846. This is barely under the 0.85 kill line, and it rests entirely on an assumed tail split. The split is also pessimistic in one respect: it assigns ranks 3–127 more than 1 byte, although varint codes them in 1 byte.

One-time and per-round costs omitted from both formulas:
- the rank→original-ID permutation (4|V| bytes), needed unless the trainer consumes rank IDs natively;
- its transfer once, or again on every profile refresh;
- the histogram and sort on the CPU;
- the offset prefix-sum.
For a matched comparison, FaBC also needs per-sequence tail offsets for parallel decoding, which Eq. 6 omits.

review.required_test:
Run an analytic byte audit from round-1 histograms on com-Amazon and com-DBLP. Compare varint (with offsets and an amortized permutation table) against:
- FaBC under both Eq. 8 readings;
- fixed-width bit-packed IDs;
- FaBC with a bit-packed tail.
Then re-evaluate on rounds 2–5 with the profile frozen. Proceed to the GPU decode benchmark only if varint beats both packed baselines and meets 0.85× FaBC.
